INT. GREENHOUSE COURTROOM - DAY

A county courtroom improvised in an old botanical greenhouse. Glass panes fog with humidity. Tomato vines climb the jury box. Benches have been dragged between rows of orchids. A gavel sits on a flowerpot.

JUDGE ELDRIDGE MARCHAND, sixties, in a robe damp at the hem, presides from a potting bench raised on cinderblocks. His spectacles keep fogging, and he wipes them with the same corner of the robe every thirty seconds.

PROSECUTOR LOUISA ZAMBRANO, thirties, stands at a lectern made from a wooden crate, her notes curling in the heat.

A DEFENDANT, forties, sits at the defense table in a borrowed suit, pale, sweating, his hands flat on the wood.

LOUISA
Your Honor, the state calls its final witness.

JUDGE
Proceed.

She turns. At the back, among the ferns, a woman rises.

LOUISA
The state calls Mrs. Dolores Vance.

DEFENDANT
(whisper, to nobody)
No.

The woman walks the aisle between the orchids. Each step leaves a wet print on the tile. She takes the stand, a wicker chair beside a fig tree, and places her hands in her lap.

JUDGE
Mrs. Vance, do you swear to tell the truth?

DOLORES
I do.

LOUISA
Mrs. Vance, do you recognize the man at the defense table?

DOLORES
I do.

LOUISA
Who is he?

DOLORES
My husband.

The Defendant closes his eyes. The Judge wipes his spectacles. A drop of condensation slides down the glass and falls onto the gavel.

LOUISA
And when did you last see him before today?

DOLORES
Eleven years ago. The night I was buried.

Silence. A moth lands on the Judge's shoulder. He does not brush it off. The Defendant opens his eyes and looks directly at her, and a very small, terrible smile crosses his face.

DEFENDANT
Hello, Dolores.
